{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310809,"nonce":"cVn33ObDt1grn74uKR+tNk0/7+I=","title":"SAME-BOX baseline (NUC, alternating engines, 5s cool-offs, median of 5, execute-only): mandelbrot rayzor 553.56ms vs zyntax 221.10ms (2.50x ...","message":"| kernel | rayzor | zyntax | ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| mandelbrot | 553.56 ms | 221.10 ms | NOT COMPARABLE — see below |\n| nbody | 492.19 ms | 380.83 ms | **1.29x behind (valid)** |\n\n**THE MANDELBROT PAIR IS NOT THE SAME PROGRAM.** VERIFIED in\n`crates/zynml/benchmarks/bench_mandelbrot.zynml`: its inner loop is four f64\nlocals (`zx, zy, zx2, zy2`), the palette is a pure function, and there is no\nComplex, no RGB, no palette array and no image array — **zero allocations**.\nRayzor's allocates two Complex per inner iteration plus a 437,500-slot image\narray. The differing checksums (zyntax 112789639 vs rayzor 112798515) are the\ntell, and it is NOT FP variation. Do not quote a mandelbrot ratio until a paired\nbenchmark exists.\n\nnbody IS comparable: their `nbody_ref` is a one-line diff from the value version\nand genuinely carries references, matching rayzor's default. Method: alternating engines so drift hits both equally,\n5s cool-off before EVERY run, median of 5, execute-only milliseconds from each\nproject's own runner.\n\nHarness: `~/sbs_bench.sh` on the nuc (`RUNS=5 COOL=5 ~/sbs_bench.sh`, results in\n`/tmp/sbs_results.txt`). Baseline written to `~/BASELINE.md`.\n\n\nThat claim came from comparing a stale rayzor CI table against a Zyntax run on\nDIFFERENT hardware. The same-box measurement refutes it. **Do not compare across\nmachines or across CI runs from different commits** — this cost two wrong\nstatements in one session.\n\n\n- Zyntax on the nuc is built WITHOUT `llvm-backend`: `llvm-sys` needs\n  `libPolly.a`, which this box lacks, and Zyntax pins inkwell without exposing\n  `force-dynamic` (rayzor works around the same problem with its own\n  `llvm-dynamic = [\"llvm-sys/force-dynamic\"]`). So the zyntax column is the\n  Cranelift tier. Their published data shows BOTH kernels flat across tiers\n  (mandelbrot 239.2 vs 238.5, nbody_ref 774.6 vs 789.5), so this is\n  representative.\n- Zyntax `origin/main` is `88459de`, matching the commit in their published\n  results JSON.\n- Their CI method, worth copying: median of 9, ONE KERNEL PER FRESH RUNNER so\n  thermal load from heavy kernels does not bleed into lighter ones.\n\n\nmandelbrot is where we lose most, and it is the allocation-heavy kernel:\nperf says free 31.6% + malloc 25.3% = 57% of runtime is the allocator, with\nJIT'd code only ~19%. See [[perf_mandelbrot_45x_tierup_regression]] for the\nsaved phi-SRA patch that takes mandelbrot 2.13s -\u003e 0.40s locally (5.3x) and the\none open question blocking it.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_rayzor_vs_zyntax_nuc_baseline` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}